From 2177a69dcd4d646e6ccd4a6787f195045423bd7c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kryxzort <101042137+Kryxzort@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 01:01:46 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 26/64] render: particle timing the way Wallpaper Engine does

Against WE 2.8.485 (addresses are wallpaper64.exe functions):

- `starttime` prewarm (GH 0x140254cc0): it was parsed as an integer and
  never read, so every rain, snow, dust and fog system started empty and
  filled in over seconds (up to minutes). It is a float, and on setup the
  system is stepped in 0.05 s steps (0.2 s from maxcount 500 up) until the
  simulated time reaches it, at least once.
- `instanceoverride.rate` is the system's playback speed: it scales the
  whole step, motion, ageing and emission alike, at least 0.01
  (GH 0x140255d00, dt *= max(rate, 0.01)). It multiplied the emitters'
  rate only, so a slowed system emitted sparsely but moved and aged at 1x.
- `instanceoverride.count` multiplies every emitter's rate (and a
  mapsequencearoundcontrolpoint's count) unless the system's flags have
  0x20 (parser bindings 0x1401f3efc..0x1401f4b44). It scaled the pool
  instead, which is `maxcount` again.
- The overrides are read every frame, so user sliders and animations on
  rate, count and lifetime are live (they were taken once at creation).
- The emitters share one timer (GH 0x14025cfe0): delay, duration, the
  random periodic windows (each "on" window reloads the instantaneous
  burst), and the burst adds to the frame's rate emission (sphererandom
  replaced it; it also ignored delay, duration and periodic emission).
- A hidden system that has particles is reset (GH 0x140255d00 ->
  0x140254f50/0x140255430): they are cleared and its emitters reloaded, so
  it starts fresh when shown and one-shot bursts replay. It used to
  freeze with stale particles and catch up 0.1 s on its first visible
  frame.
- The operators' time is the system's own clock (the sum of its steps,
  prewarm included), not the render time.

The particle alpha override is copied at spawn only in WE too
(+0x3ec, read by the spawn interpreter alone), as here.

Origin: ours
Why: particle systems started empty and the rate/count overrides meant other things, in 61 of the owner's scenes (docs/player/spikes/we-lwe-gap-audit.md B3: P1, P3, P4, P13, P14, P18, P19)
Status: candidate-for-upstream
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
